---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Working with Research Participants with Disabilities";
---
<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="meta">My take on the paper.</p>

  <div class="paper">
    <p>
      I agree with the author's skepticism in using “simulated impairments” as a research method because people who have certain impairments could rely on other senses, which is a skill that able-bodied people might not possess. In my opinion, it also normalizes that the bodies of people with disabilities should be as close to that of an able-bodied person.
    </p>

    <p>
      People who are early blind develop a stronger perception of auditory motion, which a person with blind fold would not be able to emulate.
    </p>

    <p>
      The concepts of “proxy users” and “simulated impairments” are different and should be specified as such. For instance, the proxy user interacts with the actual user and the technology used by the user, whereas simulated impairments could be directly disconnected from the impairment and/or disability the user might have.
    </p>

    <p>
      The “proxy users” research methodology can be useful for people with more than one impairment, in addition to the author’s claims, and goes to show why user and “proxy user” research is important, as the latter can provide important information to improve the living standard if the user.
    </p>

    <p>
      The author's method of case study for research really stood out to me, and it can be a good source for understanding the depth of the impairment, as well as research on users with multiple impairments.
    </p>

    <p>
      [1] Park WJ, Fine I. The perception of auditory motion in sighted and early blind individuals. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2023 Dec 5;120(49):e2310156120. doi:10.1073/pnas.2310156120.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
